---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import AverageTimeTool from "../components/AverageTimeTool.astro";

const title = "Average Time Calculator - Free Duration & Clock Time";
const description =
  "Calculate average time from durations or clock times. Bulk input, weighted & running averages, min/max/median, and CSV export. Midnight-safe and free.";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What is an average time calculator?",
    answer:
      "It is a tool that takes a set of time values and returns their mean. It also shows the total, count, minimum, maximum, median, and range so you can check the result instead of trusting a single number.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between duration and clock time?",
    answer:
      "A duration is an amount of elapsed time, such as 1 minute 30 seconds. A clock time is a point in the day, such as 11:45 PM. The two need different math when values cross midnight.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I choose between duration and clock time?",
    answer:
      "Use duration mode when each value measures how long something took, such as lap times, commutes, workouts, or handling time. Use clock time mode when each value is a point in the day, such as bedtime, arrival time, or shift start time.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I calculate average time in Excel or Google Sheets?",
    answer:
      "For durations stored as time values, use =AVERAGE(range). For weighted time, use =SUMPRODUCT(times,weights)/SUM(weights). For clock times, use a circular-mean formula that averages sine and cosine values over a 24-hour period. This page reproduces those calculations automatically.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do you calculate average time?",
    answer:
      "Convert every value to a common unit such as seconds, add them together, and divide by the number of values. This page does that instantly and supports hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds, and mixed formats.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the average time formula?",
    answer:
      "For durations, use total seconds divided by the number of values. For clock times, convert each time to an angle on a 24-hour circle, average the sine and cosine components, and convert the resulting angle back to a time.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I calculate average handling time?",
    answer:
      "Paste call durations in duration mode. If some rows represent larger groups of calls, enable weighted average and add a weight after each time. Compare the median when a few unusually long calls may distort the mean.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do you average times that cross midnight?",
    answer:
      "For clock times, the page uses circular averaging. For example, 23:50 and 00:10 average close to 00:00, not 12:00. This is essential for sleep times, arrival times, and shift times.",
  },
  {
    question: "What formats can I paste?",
    answer:
      "You can paste HH:MM:SS, MM:SS, seconds, 1h 23m 45s, 1.5h, 45.5m, and 11:45 PM. In weighted mode, add a comma or tab followed by the weight for each value.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I calculate a weighted average time?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Turn on weighted average and enter each value with a weight, such as 4m 20s,3 or 4m 20s<TAB>3. The result is the total weighted time divided by the total weight.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I calculate a running average time?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Turn on running average to see the cumulative average after the first value, second value, third value, and so on.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are min, max, median, and range in time data?",
    answer:
      "Min is the shortest value, max is the longest, median is the middle value after sorting, and range is the difference between max and min.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I export results?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Use Copy result or Download CSV. The download includes the mode, average, total, count, min, max, median, and range.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is my data stored?",
    answer:
      "No. The page runs entirely in your browser. Time values are never uploaded, stored, or sent to a server.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I average mixed time formats in the same list?",
    answer:
      "Yes. You can mix HH:MM:SS, MM:SS, seconds, decimal hours, and unit expressions such as 1h 23m 45s. Each line is converted to seconds before calculating the average.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the formula for a weighted average time?",
    answer:
      "Multiply each time by its weight, add all weighted values together, then divide by the total weight. For example, 4m 20s,3 means this value represents three observations, not one.",
  },
  {
    question: "When should I use median instead of average?",
    answer:
      "Use the median when one very long or very short value would distort the mean. For example, one unusually slow commute should not hide the typical daily travel time.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I calculate average pace from lap or split times?",
    answer:
      "Enable Average pace in duration mode and enter the distance each value covers, such as 0.4 km for a 400 m lap. The average time is divided by that distance to give the average pace per km or per mile. Note that average speed needs different math: convert each time to speed first, then average the speeds.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the calculator handle milliseconds?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The parser accepts milliseconds in formats such as 1:30.450, 90.25s, and 1.5s. It also shows milliseconds in the result when needed.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I use this for dates or time zones?",
    answer:
      "No. This tool is designed for time durations and clock times within a single day. It does not add days, convert time zones, or account for daylight saving time.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why does clock time use circular average?",
    answer:
      "A clock wraps around every 24 hours. Values near midnight are close together on a circle even though their hour numbers look far apart. Circular averaging keeps that geometry, so 23:50 and 00:10 average near 00:00.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I use the average time calculator on my phone?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The page is fully responsive and runs in any modern browser on a phone or tablet. Nothing needs to be installed, and all calculations still happen on your device.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is there a limit to how many time values I can enter?",
    answer:
      "No. You can enter any number of values, one per line. The result updates instantly regardless of list length, and CSV import makes it easy to bring in even larger lists.",
  },
  {
    question: "How accurate are the results?",
    answer:
      "Calculations run in full precision and are only rounded when displayed. Milliseconds are preserved when present, and because the math uses standard arithmetic, results match the spreadsheet formulas in the Spreadsheet section above.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I prefill the calculator from a link?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Add query parameters to the URL, for example ?mode=duration&values=1:30%0A1:25 to enter two values, or add &weighted=1, &running=1, or &format=hhmmss. Each value is separated by %0A, the URL encoding for a new line.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#organization",
      name: "Average Time Calculator",
      url: "https://average-time-calculator.com/",
      foundingDate: "2026-08-13",
    },
    {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#webpage",
      url: "https://average-time-calculator.com/",
      name: "Average Time Calculator",
      description,
      inLanguage: "en",
      datePublished: "2026-08-13",
      dateModified: "2026-08-17",
      lastReviewed: "2026-08-17",
      reviewedBy: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#organization",
      },
      mainEntity: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#app",
      },
      about: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#app",
      },
      primaryImageOfPage: {
        "@type": "ImageObject",
        url: "https://average-time-calculator.com/og-image.png",
        width: 1200,
        height: 630,
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#app",
      name: "Average Time Calculator",
      url: "https://average-time-calculator.com/",
      description,
      applicationCategory: "UtilityApplication",
      operatingSystem: "Any",
      browserRequirements: "Requires JavaScript",
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      offers: {
        "@type": "Offer",
        price: "0",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
      },
      featureList: [
        "Calculate average time",
        "Duration average",
        "Clock time average",
        "Weighted average",
        "Running average",
        "Min, max, median, and range",
        "Copy result",
        "Download CSV",
        "Import CSV",
        "Shareable result link",
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        name: faq.question,
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: faq.answer,
        },
      })),
    },
    {
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#article",
      headline: "How to Calculate Average Time from Durations and Clock Times",
      description,
      articleSection: "Average time calculator",
      inLanguage: "en",
      datePublished: "2026-08-13",
      dateModified: "2026-08-13",
      author: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#organization",
      },
      publisher: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#organization",
      },
      about: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#app",
      },
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#webpage",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "WebSite",
      "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#website",
      url: "https://average-time-calculator.com/",
      name: "Average Time Calculator",
      description,
      inLanguage: "en",
      publisher: {
        "@id": "https://average-time-calculator.com/#organization",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://average-time-calculator.com/"
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="section-pad pb-20 pt-12 sm:pt-16">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <AverageTimeTool />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad">
    <div class="page-shell grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] lg:gap-16">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-emerald-700">
          What is it?
        </p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.04em] text-zinc-950 sm:text-4xl">
          What is an average time calculator?
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50/60 p-6">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-800">
            An average time calculator finds the mean of a set of durations or clock times. For durations, convert each value to seconds, add them together, and divide by the count. For clock times, use circular averaging so values such as 23:50 and 00:10 average close to 00:00 instead of 12:00.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <table class="w-full overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-50 text-zinc-700">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Input type</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Correct method</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-200 bg-white">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Duration</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Sum all elapsed times, then divide by count.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Clock time</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Average the angles on a 24-hour circle.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-5">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-900">Why midnight changes the answer</p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 text-sm sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="rounded-md bg-rose-50 px-3 py-3 text-rose-700">
              Plain math: 23:50 + 00:10 = 12:00
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-md bg-emerald-50 px-3 py-3 text-emerald-700">
              Circular mean: 23:50 + 00:10 = 00:00
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-zinc-200 bg-white">
    <div class="page-shell section-pad">
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.9fr_1.1fr] lg:gap-16">
        <div>
          <h2 class="max-w-xl text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.04em] text-zinc-950 sm:text-4xl">
            One tool for elapsed time and time of day.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
            Most average time calculators treat every value as a duration. That works for lap times, but it can give a wrong answer when you average clock times around midnight.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-950">Duration average</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
              Add all elapsed times together, then divide by the number of values. Use it for race laps, commute time, workouts, tasks, and customer handling time.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-5 font-mono text-sm text-emerald-700">1:30 + 1:25 + 1:35 = 1:30</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-950">Clock time average</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
              Use circular averaging to find the middle point of several clock times, even when they cross midnight. Use it for bedtimes, arrivals, and shift times.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-5 font-mono text-sm text-emerald-700">23:50 + 00:10 = 00:00</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-zinc-200 bg-white">
    <div class="page-shell section-pad">
      <div class="max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.04em] text-zinc-950 sm:text-4xl">
          Why this calculator is built differently
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
          Most time-average tools only handle elapsed durations. This page separates durations from times of day, shows supporting statistics, and keeps everything in the browser.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-zinc-200">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-50 text-zinc-700">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">What you need</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Typical time-average tool</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">This calculator</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-200 bg-white">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Duration or clock time</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Durations only, so midnight is often wrong.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Both modes, with circular averaging for clock times.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Result context</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Usually one average, sometimes total and count.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Average, total, count, min, max, median, and range or spread.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Advanced averages</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Rarely available.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Weighted and running averages with examples and charts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Bulk input</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Often split into hour, minute, and second fields.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">One textarea for mixed formats, paste, and CSV import.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-zinc-900">Privacy</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">May require sign-up, ads, or server-side processing.</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-zinc-600">Free, private, no ads, and no data leaves the browser.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="formula" class="section-pad">
    <div class="page-shell grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] lg:gap-16">
      <div>
        <h2 class="max-w-xl text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.04em] text-zinc-950 sm:text-4xl">
          The formula is simple. The edge cases are not.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
          For durations, convert everything to seconds, sum the values, and divide by the count. For clock times, map each value to an angle on a 24-hour circle and average the angles.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-900">Duration average</p>
          <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-600">
            Average = Total time / Number of values
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-900">Clock time average</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-600">
            Convert each time to an angle, average the sine and cosine values, then convert the resulting angle back to a time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-900">Why midnight changes the answer</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-600">
            Plain arithmetic on 23:50 and 00:10 produces 12:00. Circular averaging correctly gives 00:00 because both values sit close together on the 24-hour clock.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="worked-examples" class="section-pad">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <div class="max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.04em] text-zinc-950 sm:text-4xl">
          How to calculate average time, with examples
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
          The same principle applies to both modes: convert every value to a common unit first. The difference is what happens after conversion. Duration data is measured on a straight line, while clock time is measured on a circle that wraps every 24 hours.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-950">Duration example: four lap times</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
            Start with a small running log. Each lap is already written as minutes and seconds, but the calculation is easiest after converting everything to seconds.
          </p>
          <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-zinc-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-800">1:32.40
1:31.20
1:33.10
1:30.85</pre>
          <table class="mt-4 w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-zinc-50 text-zinc-700">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Lap</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Seconds</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-200">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">1:32.40</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">92.40</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">1:31.20</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">91.20</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">1:33.10</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">93.10</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">1:30.85</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">90.85</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-emerald-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-emerald-900">
            <span class="font-semibold">Average = Total / Count</span><br />
            367.55 seconds / 4 = 91.8875 seconds = <span class="font-mono">1:31.8875</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-950">Clock time example: crossing midnight</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
            Two bedtimes sit close together on the clock even though their hour numbers look far apart.
          </p>
          <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-zinc-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-800">23:50
00:10</pre>
          <table class="mt-4 w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-zinc-50 text-zinc-700">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Time</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Angle</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Interpretation</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-200">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">23:50</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">357.5°</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-zinc-600">10 minutes before midnight</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">00:10</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-zinc-700">2.5°</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-zinc-600">10 minutes after midnight</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-emerald-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-emerald-900">
            Average the angles on the circle, then convert the result back to a time:
            <span class="font-mono">23:50 + 00:10 = 00:00</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-950">Why the duration method works</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
            Elapsed time behaves like any other measurement. You can add the seconds without changing their meaning, then divide by the number of observations.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-emerald-700">
            Average duration = (t₁ + t₂ + ... + tₙ) / n
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-950">Why the clock method is different</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
            A clock has no natural start point. Converting to angles and averaging sine and cosine preserves the fact that 23:50 and 00:10 are only 20 minutes apart.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-sm leading-7 text-emerald-700">
            Mean angle = atan2(Σ sin θ, Σ cos θ)
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-use" class="section-pad">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.04em] text-zinc-950 sm:text-4xl">
          How to use the average time calculator
